<p dir="auto"><h2 tabindex="-1" dir="auto">How to build</h2><a id="user-content-how-to-build" aria-label="Permalink: How to build" href="#how-to-build"></a></p>
<p dir="auto">The port isn't downloaded ready-made. It's built on your PC from your own copy.</p>
<p dir="auto"><strong>The easy way:</strong></p>
<ol dir="auto">
<li>Install the prerequisites below.</li>
<li>Download this project <strong>with its submodule</strong>: <code>git clone --recursive https://github.com/ItsNotRexed/Leonida</code>. A
"Download ZIP" from GitHub leaves out <code>third_party/rpf9-explorer</code>. The build fetches it if <code>git</code> is installed,
and otherwise tells you what to do.</li>
<li>Double-click <strong><code>Build.bat</code></strong>.</li>
</ol>
<p dir="auto">It finds your copy of the game by itself: it looks in Downloads, Desktop, Documents, Games, your home folder and
the top of every drive, two folders deep. It recognises the version from a few file sizes, without reading
anything large. If you have more than one copy it asks which one, and if it finds none it asks you to paste the
folder. Before starting it checks that everything below is installed and that there is enough free space, and it
lists anything missing in one go. The result goes to <code>Documents\Leonida</code>. A stopped build continues where it left
off.</p>
<p dir="auto">Your game folder is the one that holds <code>MicrosoftGame.Config</code> and the <code>.rpf</code> archives, as plain files.</p>
<p dir="auto"><strong>Prerequisites:</strong></p>
<ul dir="auto">
<li><strong>Your own copy</strong> of the Xbox Series package, version 1.0.2.1.</li>
<li><strong>Windows 10/11 x64</strong>, with about <strong>350 GB</strong> free for work files. They can go on another drive with <code>--work</code>.</li>
<li><a href="https://www.python.org/downloads/" rel="nofollow">Python 3.12+</a> (tick "Add python.exe to PATH").</li>
<li><a href="https://dotnet.microsoft.com/download/dotnet/8.0" rel="nofollow">.NET 8 SDK</a>.</li>
<li><a href="https://vulkan.lunarg.com/" rel="nofollow">Vulkan SDK</a>, for <code>spirv-as</code> / <code>spirv-val</code>.</li>
<li><a href="https://github.com/llvm/llvm-project/releases">LLVM 21+</a> with the <strong>AMDGPU target</strong> for <code>llvm-mc</code>. The official
Windows build has no AMDGPU target. On Windows, <code>scripts\build_llvm_mc.ps1</code> builds a native <code>llvm-mc.exe</code> with it
(no WSL; needs git and the Visual Studio C++ tools below) and sets <code>LLVM_MC</code>. Or install <code>llvm-21</code> in WSL and set
<code>LLVM_MC</code> to the whole command, <code>LLVM_MC="wsl -d Ubuntu -e /usr/lib/llvm-21/bin/llvm-mc"</code> (see
<a href="https://github.com/ItsNotRexed/Leonida/blob/main/docs/design.md#disassembling-shaders">docs/design.md</a>). On Linux, <code>llvm-mc</code> on PATH is enough.</li>
<li>Visual Studio 2022 with <strong>Desktop development with C++</strong> and the <strong>Windows SDK 10.0.26100 or newer</strong>, for the
runtime stage (the runtime uses version 1.2 root signatures, which older SDKs don't declare).</li>
<li><a href="https://ffmpeg.org/download.html" rel="nofollow">ffmpeg</a> on PATH (optional: decodes the XMA2 streams, and required for <code>--wav</code>).</li>
</ul>
<p dir="auto"><strong>From a terminal</strong>, with the same behaviour and more control:</p>
<div dir="auto" data-snippet-clipboard-copy-content="python -I make_game.py --find                          # list the copies found on this PC
python -I make_game.py --check                         # check your copy, build nothing
python -I make_game.py --game &quot;D:\path\to\your\package&quot; --out &quot;D:\Games\Leonida&quot; --work &quot;E:\leonida-work&quot;
python -I make_game.py --stages shaders,assets         # only some stages
python -I make_game.py --game &quot;D:\path\to\your\package&quot; --wav   # also decode Opus to PCM WAV (large)
python -I make_game.py --wave64-emulate off            # auto (default), on or off; see below"><pre>python <span>-</span>I make_game.py <span>--</span>find                          <span><span>#</span> list the copies found on this PC</span>
python <span>-</span>I make_game.py <span>--</span>check                         <span><span>#</span> check your copy, build nothing</span>
python <span>-</span>I make_game.py <span>--</span>game <span><span>"</span>D:\path\to\your\package<span>"</span></span> <span>--</span>out <span><span>"</span>D:\Games\Leonida<span>"</span></span> <span>--</span>work <span><span>"</span>E:\leonida-work<span>"</span></span>
python <span>-</span>I make_game.py <span>--</span>stages shaders<span>,</span>assets         <span><span>#</span> only some stages</span>
python <span>-</span>I make_game.py <span>--</span>game <span><span>"</span>D:\path\to\your\package<span>"</span></span> <span>--</span>wav   <span><span>#</span> also decode Opus to PCM WAV (large)</span>
python <span>-</span>I make_game.py <span>--</span>wave64<span>-</span>emulate off            <span><span>#</span> auto (default), on or off; see below</span></pre></div>
<p dir="auto"><code>--wave64-emulate</code> decides whether wave64 compute and mesh shaders are translated to be exact on any subgroup size
(see "Wave64 emulation" in <a href="https://github.com/ItsNotRexed/Leonida/blob/main/docs/design.md">docs/design.md</a>). NVIDIA and Intel GPUs have 32-wide subgroups and need
it. The default, <code>auto</code>, asks <code>vulkaninfo</code> (part of the Vulkan SDK) and turns it on unless every GPU reports 64-wide
subgroups, or when it can't tell; it also turns it on when the GPU the runtime would use can't be made to run
compute and mesh shaders 64-wide. The mode is saved next to the translated shaders, and changing it translates them
again. With <code>auto</code>, a work folder that previously used emulation keeps it for later translations and retries, even if
the current GPU supports native wave64. Use <code>--wave64-emulate off</code> to translate it natively.</p>
<p dir="auto">The build records which translator sources produced the shaders. A change to those files reruns translation using the
saved disassembly. Older work folders need one translation after upgrading to record this information.</p>
<p dir="auto">Stages run in order: <code>check</code>, <code>shaders</code>, <code>assets</code>, <code>runtime</code>, <code>pipelines</code> (creates every translated shader on your
GPU driver and saves a pipeline cache, so the first boot doesn't compile them). The shader translation is the longest step: it runs
on <code>--jobs</code> processes (half the CPU threads by default).</p>
<p dir="auto"><code>--wav</code> is off by default because 16-bit PCM WAV is several times larger than Ogg Opus. It decodes the repackaged Ogg
Opus streams to <code>work\assets\audio\wav</code> after the audio bank step; existing WAV files are skipped when a build resumes.</p>
<p dir="auto"><h3 tabindex="-1" dir="auto">Vulkan diagnostics on Linux</h3><a id="user-content-vulkan-diagnostics-on-linux" aria-label="Permalink: Vulkan diagnostics on Linux" href="#vulkan-diagnostics-on-linux"></a></p>
<p dir="auto">With CMake 3.24+, a C++20 compiler, and Vulkan development headers and loader installed:</p>
<div dir="auto" data-snippet-clipboard-copy-content="cmake -S runtime -B runtime/build/linux
cmake --build runtime/build/linux --parallel
./runtime/build/linux/vk_pipeline_check /path/to/spirv"><pre>cmake -S runtime -B runtime/build/linux
cmake --build runtime/build/linux --parallel
./runtime/build/linux/vk_pipeline_check /path/to/spirv</pre></div>
<p dir="auto">Pass the directory containing <code>index.json</code> and the translated SPIR-V files to <code>vk_pipeline_check</code>.</p>
<p dir="auto"><code>make_game.py</code> runs on Linux too. The <code>pipelines</code> stage uses the <code>vk_pipeline_check</code> built above
(<code>runtime/build/linux</code>, or <code>runtime/build</code>). The <code>runtime</code> stage builds on Windows only, so on Linux it needs a
Windows build of <code>runtime/</code>; without one it is left out of the default stages, and asking for it fails before any
work starts.</p>
<p dir="auto"><h3 tabindex="-1" dir="auto">Running the loader on Linux</h3><a id="user-content-running-the-loader-on-linux" aria-label="Permalink: Running the loader on Linux" href="#running-the-loader-on-linux"></a></p>
<p dir="auto">The loader is a Windows program and runs under Wine or Proton; CI runs its tests under Wine on every change to
<code>runtime/</code> (the <code>wine</code> job of the Runtime workflow). Give the <code>runtime</code> stage a folder with <code>gta6rt.exe</code> and
<code>gta6rt_tls_host.dll</code>, such as the <code>runtime-windows</code> artifact of that workflow's <code>windows</code> job, unzipped:</p>
<div dir="auto" data-snippet-clipboard-copy-content="python3 -I make_game.py --runtime-build ~/Downloads/runtime-windows"><pre>python3 -I make_game.py --runtime-build <span>~</span>/Downloads/runtime-windows</pre></div>
<p dir="auto">It copies both files into <code>--out</code> and writes <code>gta6rt.sh</code> next to them, which starts <code>gta6rt.exe</code> with <code>wine</code>, or
with the Wine named by <code>WINE</code> (for Proton, its <code>files/bin/wine</code>). The same option skips the build on Windows.</p>
<p dir="auto"><h2 tabindex="-1" dir="auto">How it works</h2><a id="user-content-how-it-works" aria-label="Permalink: How it works" href="#how-it-works"></a></p>
<markdown-accessiblity-table><table>
<thead>
<tr>
<th>Piece</th>
<th>What it does</th>
<th>Where</th>
</tr>
</thead>
<tbody>
<tr>
<td>Version check</td>
<td>Every package file's name, size and SHA-256 for each supported version</td>
<td><code>game/</code>, <code>make_game.py</code></td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td>Shader pipeline</td>
<td>Carves DXBC containers from <code>shaders.rpf</code> without its encrypted table of contents. Unpacks the <code>XBCH</code> → <code>FSSM</code> native RDNA2 program, disassembles it with LLVM (<code>gfx1013</code>) and translates it to SPIR-V</td>
<td><code>tools/shader_carve.py</code>, <code>fssm_unpack.py</code>, <code>shader_disasm.py</code>, <code>rdna2spirv.py</code></td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td>Asset export</td>
<td>Finds validated resources inside RPF9 archives and the loose <code>.ttpf</code> texture pools</td>
<td><code>tools/rpf9dump</code> (on rpf9-explorer's Core)</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td>Runtime</td>
<td>Maps the PE image, resolves imports to shims (GDK, XMem, <code>d3d12_xs</code>)</td>
<td><code>runtime/</code></td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td>Research tools</td>
<td>PE/RTTI inspection, raw carving, texture tiling probes, GPU test harnesses</td>
<td><code>tools/</code>, <code>runtime/test/</code></td>
</tr>
</tbody>
</table></markdown-accessiblity-table>
<p dir="auto">The translator's runtime contract (user SGPRs, push constants, memory model, bindless images, vertex fetch,
system values) and all the format notes are in <a href="https://github.com/ItsNotRexed/Leonida/blob/main/docs/design.md">docs/design.md</a>; the format, asset, runtime
and validation notes are indexed in <a href="https://github.com/ItsNotRexed/Leonida/blob/main/docs/README.md">docs/README.md</a>. The Xbox D3D12 plan is in
<a href="https://github.com/ItsNotRexed/Leonida/blob/main/docs/d3d12_vulkan.md">docs/d3d12_vulkan.md</a>. Audio bank formats and the export tools are in
<a href="https://github.com/ItsNotRexed/Leonida/blob/main/docs/audio.md">docs/audio.md</a>; <a href="https://github.com/ItsNotRexed/Leonida/blob/main/docs/related_projects.md">docs/related_projects.md</a> records the prior art we may
read and the licence limits on it.</p>
<p dir="auto"><h2 tabindex="-1" dir="auto">Want to help?</h2><a id="user-content-want-to-help" aria-label="Permalink: Want to help?" href="#want-to-help"></a></p>
<p dir="auto">The biggest open pieces:</p>
<ul dir="auto">
<li><strong>Shader translator on hardware</strong>: compute, mesh and geometry now translate (see the translator contract in
<a href="https://github.com/ItsNotRexed/Leonida/blob/main/docs/design.md">docs/design.md</a>); still open are the vertex-shader input layout (<code>v0</code> = <code>VertexIndex</code> is
unverified if the console runs VS as NGG) and wave64 cross-half code in VS/PS/GS on 32-wide subgroups.</li>
<li><strong>Xbox D3D12 → Vulkan</strong>: the <code>d3d12_xs</code> command list and descriptor heap model.</li>
<li><strong>Model layouts</strong> the decoder rejects today.</li>
</ul>
<p dir="auto">See <a href="https://github.com/ItsNotRexed/Leonida/blob/main/CONTRIBUTING.md">CONTRIBUTING.md</a>. Never attach game files, extracted assets or generated shaders to issues or
pull requests.</p>
<p dir="auto"><h2 tabindex="-1" dir="auto">Credits</h2><a id="user-content-credits" aria-label="Permalink: Credits" href="#credits"></a></p>
<ul dir="auto">
<li><a href="https://github.com/breadbbbb/rpf9-explorer">rpf9-explorer</a> by breadbbbb: the RPF9 model, texture-pool and AWC
decoders used by <code>tools/rpf9dump</code> (git submodule).</li>
<li><a href="https://gitlab.freedesktop.org/mesa/mesa" rel="nofollow">Mesa</a> (MIT): GFX10 resource-descriptor layout (<code>tools/gfx10_formats.json</code>),
and the ACO/RADV rules used as reference for 16-bit results and shader system registers.</li>
<li><a href="https://github.com/microsoft/libHttpClient">libHttpClient</a> (MIT) by Microsoft: the XTaskQueue/XAsync implementation
in <code>third_party/xtaskqueue</code>, used by the offline <code>xgameruntime</code> shim.</li>
<li><a href="https://llvm.org/" rel="nofollow">LLVM</a>: AMDGPU disassembler.</li>
<li>Logo: <a href="https://www.steamgriddb.com/icon/122618" rel="nofollow">"Grand Theft Auto VI" icon by SlyDevil</a> on SteamGridDB.</li>
<li><a href="https://github.com/KhronosGroup/SPIRV-Tools">SPIRV-Tools</a>: assembly and validation.</li>
</ul>
<p dir="auto">Grand Theft Auto and all related names are trademarks of their owners. This project is not affiliated with or
endorsed by them.</p>
