        <item>
            <title><![CDATA[Inkling-2-Mini in 5.4 GB Memory (408 pts)]]></title>
            <link>https://github.com/ShinMegamiBoson/lookatmyquant</link>
            <guid>50591853</guid>
            <pubDate>Sat, 21 Nov 2026 18:02:19 GMT</pubDate>
            <description><![CDATA[<p>URL: <a href="https://github.com/ShinMegamiBoson/lookatmyquant">https://github.com/ShinMegamiBoson/lookatmyquant</a>, See on <a href="https://news.ycombinator.com/item?id=50591853">Hacker News</a></p>
<div id="readability-page-1" class="page"><div data-hpc="true" containertiming="hpc"><article itemprop="text">
<p dir="auto">LookAtMyQuant is a quantization compiler. It measures how much each stored piece of a model hurts the output when compressed. It then picks a code for each piece so the whole model fits a size budget with the least damage.</p>
<p dir="auto">On Gemma 4 E2B, its GGUF has 12× lower KL than Q4_K_M at the same file size. The method and every result are on <a href="https://lookatmyquant.pages.dev/" rel="nofollow">the site</a>.</p>
<p dir="auto">The code is under the MIT license.</p>
<p dir="auto"><h2 tabindex="-1" dir="auto">LookAtMyQuant 2: Qwen3.5-4B in 1.2 GB</h2><a id="user-content-lookatmyquant-2-qwen35-4b-in-12-gb" aria-label="Permalink: LookAtMyQuant 2: Qwen3.5-4B in 1.2 GB" href="#lookatmyquant-2-qwen35-4b-in-12-gb"></a></p>
<p dir="auto"><a href="https://lookatmyquant2.pages.dev/" rel="nofollow">LookAtMyQuant 2</a> takes Qwen3.5-4B to 1.23 GB, 2.3 bits per weight. The file solves 330 of 540 exact-answer math problems. Prism ML's Bonsai 2 Ternary 4B solves 328 at 1.20 GB, and the original solves 359. Its KL from the original on held-out text is 0.45 nats, against 0.59 for Bonsai.</p>
<p dir="auto">The recipe is post-training quantization with trellis codes, calibrated on the model's own worked math solutions with digit tokens weighted 10×, and with bits allocated per row by Fisher information. 4,000 steps of distillation follow, in which every layer is re-quantized once from the original weights. The page has the findings, the full recipe and its cost, about $34 of GPU time.</p>
<p dir="auto">The recipe's code is here, and the next section runs it. The 1.23 GB file itself is the <a href="https://github.com/ShinMegamiBoson/lookatmyquant/releases/tag/lm2-qwen35-4b-1.2gb"><code>lm2-qwen35-4b-1.2gb</code> release</a>, packed for the Apple Silicon runtime.</p>
<p dir="auto"><h2 tabindex="-1" dir="auto">Qwen3.8-27B in 7.2 GB</h2><a id="user-content-qwen38-27b-in-72-gb" aria-label="Permalink: Qwen3.8-27B in 7.2 GB" href="#qwen38-27b-in-72-gb"></a></p>
<p dir="auto">The same recipe takes Qwen3.8-27B from 53.7 GB to 7.2 GB, 2.1 bits per weight, calibrated and distilled on English prose and chat, code and math. <a href="https://huggingface.co/shinmegamiboson/Qwen3.8-27B-LookAtMyQuant-7.2GB" rel="nofollow">The file</a> solves 401 of the 540 math problems, as many as the original, and Unsloth's <code>UD-IQ2_XXS</code> GGUF of the same size solves 348. It also scores above that GGUF on HumanEval (73.8% against 67.1%), MMLU (78.8% against 75.0%) and GSM8K (94.5% against 92.7%).</p>
<p dir="auto">Beside it sits <a href="https://huggingface.co/shinmegamiboson/Qwen3.8-27B-LookAtMyQuant-7.2GB-drafter" rel="nofollow">a 4-bit DSpine drafter</a> (1.02 GB, converted from <code>tencent/Qwen3.8-27B-DSpine-b16</code> with <code>runtime_mlx/convert_dspine.py</code> and packed with <code>pack_drafter.py</code>), which roughly doubles decode speed on math and code on Apple silicon.</p>
<p dir="auto"><h2 tabindex="-1" dir="auto">Inkling-2-Mini in 5.4 GB and 8.9 GB</h2><a id="user-content-inkling-2-mini-in-54-gb-and-89-gb" aria-label="Permalink: Inkling-2-Mini in 5.4 GB and 8.9 GB" href="#inkling-2-mini-in-54-gb-and-89-gb"></a></p>
<p dir="auto">The same compiler and proximal fine-tuning (<code>SQYFR</code>), extended to a 26.4B physical / 5.4B active 3×-looped MoE (<code>L6–17</code> looped 3× = 48 logical visits, 128 experts top-8), compresses <a href="https://huggingface.co/thinkingmachines/Inkling-2-Mini" rel="nofollow"><code>thinkingmachines/Inkling-2-Mini</code></a> from 18.89 GB of text weights to <strong><a href="https://huggingface.co/shinmegamiboson/Inkling-2-Mini-LookAtMyQuant-5.4GB" rel="nofollow">5.38 GB (<code>1.63</code> bits/weight)</a></strong> and <strong><a href="https://huggingface.co/shinmegamiboson/Inkling-2-Mini-LookAtMyQuant-8.9GB" rel="nofollow">8.87 GB (<code>2.69</code> bits/weight)</a></strong> using 4 KB SRAM hashed block-vector trellis codes (<code>ht2</code>/<code>ht3</code>), row trellis codes (<code>rtr1</code>/<code>rtr4</code>), and a <code>tr2</code> floor on the tail <code>lm_head</code> bucket (<code>head.7</code>).</p>
<p dir="auto">Evaluated on the same BF16 teacher, <code>openai/gsm8k</code> (<code>test[:200]</code>, greedy, <code>reasoning_effort="none"</code>), all <code>164</code> executable <code>openai/openai_humaneval</code> problems, <code>4</code> Vision VQA tests (with the untouched 637 MB BF16 vision tower), and 16+13-language TextMix windows:</p>
