<td><code>lm2Finetune</code></td>
<td><code>finetune.py</code></td>
<td>4,000 steps of distillation on latent weights pulled toward their codes, with every layer re-quantized once from the original weights</td>
<td>2.7 hours, B200</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td><code>lm2Math</code>, <code>lm2Math216</code></td>
<td><code>math_eval.py</code></td>
<td>The 540-problem benchmark and a second set of 216, for the original, Bonsai, the build and the fine-tuned build</td>
<td>20 minutes a model, H100</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td><code>lm2TextKL</code></td>
<td><code>text_kl.py</code></td>
<td>KL from the original on WikiText-2 and on the text mix's 13 sources</td>
<td>1 minute a model, H100</td>
</tr>
</tbody>
</table></markdown-accessiblity-table>
<p dir="auto"><code>lm2BuildSmoke</code>, <code>lm2FinetuneSmoke</code> and <code>lm2MathSmoke</code> run the same code on two layers, 60 steps and 36 problems, in a few minutes each.</p>
<p dir="auto"><strong>The row plan.</strong> <code>lm2Build</code> reads <code>results/compiler/rows/q35_rowplan_1p2GB.json</code>, which gives 1 to 4 bits to each of 1,520 row groups. <code>row_allocation.py</code> made it. Its <code>build-control</code> command builds and packs a control with a fixed split of rows, <code>prepare-rows</code> allocates rows inside the control's packed bytes with <code>rowgroup_alloc.py</code>, and <code>build-rows</code> builds under the plan and checks the packed size against it.</p>
<p dir="auto"><strong>The codebooks.</strong> The four e-means codebooks the build decodes with are in <code>cache/codes/</code>. <code>--trellis-fit lloyd</code> gives the earlier ones, and a plan made before the change keeps them unless the flag says otherwise.</p>
<p dir="auto"><strong>Recent equal-byte recipe improvements.</strong> Two zero-byte additions are available in the compiler and fine-tuning scripts:</p>
<ul dir="auto">
<li><strong>Softmax-Fisher token table ranking (<code>--embed-order sfisher-mix</code> in <code>compiler.py measure</code> and <code>quantize.py</code>).</strong> Ranks tied embedding/output buckets by a 50/50 blend of output Softmax-Fisher <code>E[p_v (1 - p_v)]</code> and corpus frequency (and untied output heads by Softmax-Fisher alone).</li>
<li><strong>Mid-training equal-byte row reallocation (<code>--realloc-at 1000</code> in <code>finetune.py</code>).</strong> Paired with <code>--rebuild-at 1000</code>, measures each layer's continuous-vs-stored proximal KL gap <code>KL(all P) - KL(layer = W)</code> to shift bits toward deep unabsorbed layers while keeping exact file size (<code>--realloc-max-delta 1</code>). Combined with <code>sfisher-mix</code> on Qwen3.5-4B at identical 1.23 GB (<code>2.297 bpp</code>), WikiText-2 KL fell from 0.425 to 0.387 (−9.0%) and 13-source KL from 0.447 to 0.378 (−15.5%, improving all 13 sources).</li>
</ul>
<p dir="auto"><strong>Where these jobs differ from our run.</strong> Our GSM8K solutions were written earlier, with thinking always off. Our text KL on 13 sources used 52 windows picked from the text mix by a separate tool; <code>--sources mix</code> takes the mix's own evaluation windows, on which our file scores 0.44 where the page reports 0.45.</p>
<p dir="auto"><h2 tabindex="-1" dir="auto">Large models run on Modal, one GPU per job</h2><a id="user-content-large-models-run-on-modal-one-gpu-per-job" aria-label="Permalink: Large models run on Modal, one GPU per job" href="#large-models-run-on-modal-one-gpu-per-job"></a></p>
<p dir="auto"><code>run_batch.py</code> names every job, and <code>modal_app.py</code> runs them. <code>modal_fetch.py</code> downloads models and the text mix used for calibration and evaluation.</p>
<div dir="auto" data-snippet-clipboard-copy-content="modal run modal_app.py::setup
modal run --detach modal_fetch.py --text
modal run --detach modal_fetch.py --models google/gemma-4-E2B-it
modal run --detach modal_app.py --jobs ccMG4E2Noise,ccMG4E2Spot,ccMG4E2EmbKv"><pre>modal run modal_app.py::setup
modal run --detach modal_fetch.py --text
modal run --detach modal_fetch.py --models google/gemma-4-E2B-it
modal run --detach modal_app.py --jobs ccMG4E2Noise,ccMG4E2Spot,ccMG4E2EmbKv</pre></div>
<p dir="auto">The runs behind the site used these machines, each with 8 CPU cores:</p>
<markdown-accessiblity-table><table>
<thead>
<tr>
<th>Model</th>
<th>GPU</th>
<th>Time</th>
</tr>
</thead>
<tbody>
<tr>
<td>SmolLM2-360M</td>
<td>L40S</td>
<td>About 50 minutes to measure, in 9 parallel jobs</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td>Gemma 4 E2B, measuring</td>
<td>H100</td>
<td>7–26 minutes per job, in 4 jobs</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td>Gemma 4 E2B, building a plan</td>
<td>H100</td>
<td>28–58 minutes per plan</td>
</tr>
</tbody>
</table></markdown-accessiblity-table>
<p dir="auto"><code>gguf_compiler.py</code> makes plans out of llama.cpp's own tensor types, so the result runs in llama.cpp as it is. <code>public_eval.py</code> scores public quantizations the same way as our plans. Both run inside the image in <code>modal_gguf.py</code>, which builds llama.cpp with CUDA.</p>
<p dir="auto"><h2 tabindex="-1" dir="auto">The Apple Silicon runtime reads Lloyd–Max and trellis plans</h2><a id="user-content-the-apple-silicon-runtime-reads-lloydmax-and-trellis-plans" aria-label="Permalink: The Apple Silicon runtime reads Lloyd–Max and trellis plans" href="#the-apple-silicon-runtime-reads-lloydmax-and-trellis-plans"></a></p>
<p dir="auto"><code>quantize.py --export &lt;folder&gt;</code> writes a plan in the format <code>runtime_mlx/</code> reads. A plan with trellis codes also needs <code>--trellis-rows 16,8</code>, which runs each trellis path along a row of its matrix, so that decoding an entry reads only that row. On Apple silicon, <code>pip install .</code> installs <code>mlx</code> and <code>mlx-lm</code>; <code>requirements.txt</code> holds the PyTorch compiler dependencies.</p>
<p dir="auto">On Gemma 4 E2B at 2.5 GB, the trellis plan has 26% lower KL than the Lloyd–Max plan and decodes about 22% slower. <code>runtime_mlx/test_trellis_gen.py</code> and <code>runtime_mlx/test_trellis.py</code> check the trellis kernels against the matrices the quantizer built.</p>
<div dir="auto" data-snippet-clipboard-copy-content="python runtime_mlx/bench.py lamq path/to/export"><pre>python runtime_mlx/bench.py lamq path/to/export</pre></div>
<p dir="auto"><h2 tabindex="-1" dir="auto">A drafter makes the 1.2 GB file generate 1.5 to 2 times faster on Apple silicon</h2><a id="user-content-a-drafter-makes-the-12-gb-file-generate-15-to-2-times-faster-on-apple-silicon" aria-label="Permalink: A drafter makes the 1.2 GB file generate 1.5 to 2 times faster on Apple silicon" href="#a-drafter-makes-the-12-gb-file-generate-15-to-2-times-faster-on-apple-silicon"></a></p>
<p dir="auto"><code>runtime_mlx/fast_q35.py</code> decodes the packed 1.2 GB Qwen3.5-4B file one token at a time, straight from its trellis codes. <code>runtime_mlx/drafter_q35.py</code> adds speculative decoding. A small drafter proposes the seven tokens after the model's latest one in a single pass. The model then checks all eight positions in one pass over its coded weights (<code>runtime_mlx/block_q35.py</code>) and keeps the proposals that match its own choice, so the text is exactly its own greedy text.</p>
<p dir="auto">On an M5 Max, over 30 held-out prompts of up to 256 tokens, generation with the drafter was 2.0 times as fast: 176 tokens a second against 88, timed in alternation in one process on 2026-11-11. A check kept 4.3 tokens on average. Over eight everyday prompts it was 1.5 times as fast. The gain follows how predictable the text is: 2.5 times on algebra, 2.0 on code, 1.2 on chat and 1.05 on a story, and one of the 30 held-out prompts ran slower with the drafter. The text matched plain greedy decoding on every prompt. These are speeds after the first token; reading the prompt takes about 0.1 s either way.</p>
<p dir="auto">Two later changes are in this code. Each was measured against the version before it on 40 paired generations: proposing from the 96,033 most common tokens was 6.0% faster, and picking the winning token inside the packed product was 5.3% faster. Linear mode also enables self-calibrating adaptive fallback by default (<code>adaptive=True</code>; pass <code>--no-adaptive</code> to disable): when rolling acceptance drops below the measured break-even ratio of drafted-check latency to serial-step latency (accounting for cache flush and drafter context replay), it pauses drafting for short exact serial bursts and resumes once acceptance recovers, preserving bit-exact greedy parity while avoiding slowdowns on low-acceptance prose.</p>
<p dir="auto">The drafter is a DSpine model, trained with <a href="https://github.com/sgl-project/SpecForge">SpecForge</a> from the published DFlash drafter for Qwen3.5-4B on the 1.2 GB file's own greedy answers. <code>modal_drafter.py</code> runs the training as one Modal job, and it cost $42. On 300 held-out prompts the trained drafter gets 4.65 tokens per check, against 4.30 for the stock DFlash drafter.</p>
<p dir="auto"><code>pack_drafter.py</code> stores the drafter at 4 bits a weight in the build's own code, with no calibration data: 464 MB against 1.83 GB at 16 bits. On 60 held-out prompts the 4-bit drafter kept 4.35 tokens per check against 4.38, and the text was the same. <code>drafter_q35.py</code> loads either form and writes the packed one out dense as it loads. The 4-bit drafter is the <a href="https://github.com/ShinMegamiBoson/lookatmyquant/releases/tag/lm2-drafter-4bit"><code>lm2-drafter-4bit</code> release</a>. It runs beside the packed 1.2 GB file, the <a href="https://github.com/ShinMegamiBoson/lookatmyquant/releases/tag/lm2-qwen35-4b-1.2gb"><code>lm2-qwen35-4b-1.2gb</code> release</a>.</p>
<div dir="auto" data-snippet-clipboard-copy-content="gh release download lm2-qwen35-4b-1.2gb --repo ShinMegamiBoson/lookatmyquant -D q35-1.2gb
gh release download lm2-drafter-4bit --repo ShinMegamiBoson/lookatmyquant -D drafter-4bit
python runtime_mlx/bench_drafted.py q35-1.2gb drafter-4bit"><pre>gh release download lm2-qwen35-4b-1.2gb --repo ShinMegamiBoson/lookatmyquant -D q35-1.2gb
gh release download lm2-drafter-4bit --repo ShinMegamiBoson/lookatmyquant -D drafter-4bit
python runtime_mlx/bench_drafted.py q35-1.2gb drafter-4bit</pre></div>
<p dir="auto">The benchmark defaults to linear verification. Use <code>--mode chat-fork</code> to try a second candidate at an ambiguous early position, or <code>--mode copy-wide</code> for known contiguous copies. Copy mode uses the source cursor and tunes the gate/up and down projections for 16-slot checks. Both modes verify all emitted tokens with the packed target. To benchmark the chat fork, run:</p>
<div dir="auto" data-snippet-clipboard-copy-content="python runtime_mlx/bench_drafted.py q35-1.2gb drafter-4bit --mode chat-fork --prompts chat"><pre>python runtime_mlx/bench_drafted.py q35-1.2gb drafter-4bit --mode chat-fork --prompts chat</pre></div>
<p dir="auto">For a copy benchmark, pass <code>--mode copy-wide --heldout path/to/copy-prompts.json</code>. The JSON entries carry <code>source</code>, <code>prompt_len</code>, and <code>input_ids</code> from the packed model's tokenizer.</p>
<p dir="auto">The checks, and the packer for a drafter of your own:</p>
<div dir="auto" data-snippet-clipboard-copy-content="python runtime_mlx/test_fast_q35.py
LAMQ_Q35_PACKED=path/to/packed python runtime_mlx/test_block_q35.py
python runtime_mlx/check_drafter.py path/to/packed path/to/drafter
python runtime_mlx/bench_drafted.py path/to/packed path/to/drafter --heldout path/to/drafter/heldout/heldout.json --take 30
python pack_drafter.py path/to/drafter path/to/drafter-4bit"><pre>python runtime_mlx/test_fast_q35.py
LAMQ_Q35_PACKED=path/to/packed python runtime_mlx/test_block_q35.py
python runtime_mlx/check_drafter.py path/to/packed path/to/drafter
python runtime_mlx/bench_drafted.py path/to/packed path/to/drafter --heldout path/to/drafter/heldout/heldout.json --take 30
python pack_drafter.py path/to/drafter path/to/drafter-4bit</pre></div>
<p dir="auto"><code>runtime_mlx/pack_dense.py</code> writes the packed file from the fine-tuned build's dense weights, and <code>runtime_mlx/modal_pack.py</code> runs it on Modal. <code>check_drafter.py</code> compares the drafter in MLX with SpecForge's own model on reference blocks that <code>drafter/reference.py</code> writes.</p>
<p dir="auto"><h2 tabindex="-1" dir="auto">Where everything is</h2><a id="user-content-where-everything-is" aria-label="Permalink: Where everything is" href="#where-everything-is"></a></p>
