<p dir="auto"><code>runtime_mlx/test_core.py</code> checks the runtime against the quantizer's math. Run <code>python runtime_mlx/make_ref.py</code> first, which writes 1.6 GB of reference files.</p>
<p dir="auto"><code>runtime_mlx/test_fast_q35.py</code> and <code>runtime_mlx/test_block_q35.py</code> check the Qwen3.5 kernels against NumPy products of independently unpacked codes, with no model. When <code>LAMQ_Q35_PACKED</code> names a packed file, the second also checks a block of tokens against decoding them one at a time. When <code>LAMQ_Q38_PACKED</code> names the packed Qwen3.8-27B file, it runs the same checks on a model whose output head is stored apart from its embedding.</p>
<p dir="auto"><h2 tabindex="-1" dir="auto">What this repository leaves out</h2><a id="user-content-what-this-repository-leaves-out" aria-label="Permalink: What this repository leaves out" href="#what-this-repository-leaves-out"></a></p>
<ul dir="auto">
<li><strong>Model weights.</strong> Everything downloads from Hugging Face. The Qwen3.5-4B jobs expect that model in <code>models/Qwen3.5-4B</code> on the volume.</li>
<li><strong>Benchmark copies.</strong> <code>eval_tasks.py</code> downloads its benchmarks. On an offline machine it reads exported copies from <code>data/bench/</code>, which are not here. The Modal images run offline, so cache the benchmarks on the volume first.</li>
<li><strong>The script that assembled <code>site/data.json</code>.</strong> It was a set of one-off steps. The source numbers are all in <code>results/</code>.</li>
<li><strong>LookAtMyQuant 2's dense weights and result files.</strong> The 1.2 GB file is a release in the packed format. The dense weights the benchmarks ran on are not published, and neither are the per-problem results behind <code>site2/data.json</code> or the script that writes the page's numbers from them.</li>
<li><strong>The drafter at 16 bits.</strong> The drafter as trained is 1.8 GB and is not published. The 4-bit one is a release file, and <code>modal_drafter.py</code> trains it.</li>
</ul>
