<h2>What was verified</h2>
<p>Five theorems, proved in the submission's <code>Main.lean</code>. Their statements are below exactly as they appear in the challenge file, which imports only Mathlib. All the mathematical notions in them (<code>riemannZeta</code>, <code>DirichletCharacter</code>, <code>DirichletCharacter.LFunction</code>, <code>IsPrimitive</code>) are Mathlib's.</p>

<p><strong>1. The Riemann Hypothesis</strong>, using Mathlib's own definition (<code>Mathlib/NumberTheory/LSeries/RiemannZeta.lean</code>): every zero of ζ other than the trivial zeros −2, −4, −6, … has real part 1/2. The condition <code>s ≠ 1</code> is there because Mathlib assigns ζ a conventional value at its pole.</p>
<pre>def RiemannHypothesis : Prop :=
  ∀ (s : ℂ) (_ : riemannZeta s = 0) (_ : ¬∃ n : ℕ, s = -2 * (n + 1)) (_ : s ≠ 1), s.re = 1 / 2

theorem riemannHypothesis : RiemannHypothesis</pre>

<p><strong>2. The same statement written out</strong> in the submission's own words. Lean accepts the two as equal by definition.</p>
<pre>theorem riemannZeta_eq_zero_re :
  ∀ s : ℂ, riemannZeta s = 0 → (¬∃ n : ℕ, s = -2 * ((n : ℂ) + 1)) → s ≠ 1 → s.re = 1 / 2</pre>

<p><strong>3. No zeros to the right of the critical line, for every Dirichlet L-function.</strong> For every modulus <code>N ≥ 1</code> and every Dirichlet character χ modulo N, primitive or not, L(χ, s) ≠ 0 whenever Re s &gt; 1/2. The one excluded case is the trivial character at <code>s = 1</code>, where the L-function has its pole.</p>
<pre>theorem LFunction_ne_zero_of_half_lt_re :
  ∀ (N : ℕ) [NeZero N] (χ : DirichletCharacter ℂ N) (s : ℂ),
    1 / 2 &lt; s.re → (χ ≠ 1 ∨ s ≠ 1) → DirichletCharacter.LFunction χ s ≠ 0</pre>

<p><strong>4. Zeros in the critical strip lie on the critical line, for every Dirichlet L-function.</strong></p>
<pre>theorem LFunction_eq_zero_re :
  ∀ (N : ℕ) [NeZero N] (χ : DirichletCharacter ℂ N) (s : ℂ),
    0 &lt; s.re → s.re &lt; 1 → DirichletCharacter.LFunction χ s = 0 → s.re = 1 / 2</pre>

<p><strong>5. The same for primitive characters</strong>, the form in which the Generalized Riemann Hypothesis for Dirichlet L-functions is usually stated.</p>
<pre>theorem LFunction_eq_zero_re_of_isPrimitive :
  ∀ (N : ℕ) [NeZero N] (χ : DirichletCharacter ℂ N), χ.IsPrimitive →
    ∀ s : ℂ, 0 &lt; s.re → s.re &lt; 1 → DirichletCharacter.LFunction χ s = 0 → s.re = 1 / 2</pre>

<p><strong>Scope.</strong> "GRH" here means Dirichlet L-functions: all moduli and all characters. Dedekind zeta functions, Hecke L-functions and automorphic L-functions in general are not among the statements that were checked.</p>

<h2>Arithmetic consequences</h2>
<p>As a check that does not depend on Mathlib's definition of the zeta function, the proof was combined with <a href="https://github.com/kimihiro64/Robin1984">kimihiro64/Robin1984</a> (commit <code>acab1a3</code>), an independent formalization of Robin's and Lagarias's criteria on the same Lean and Mathlib versions. Importing both gives two theorems about the divisor sum, with no hypotheses and no mention of zeta (<a href="https://math.mit.edu/~drew/grh/ArithSolution.lean">ArithSolution.lean</a>; statements alone in <a href="https://math.mit.edu/~drew/grh/ArithChallenge.lean">ArithChallenge.lean</a>, which imports only Mathlib):</p>
<pre>theorem robin_inequality :
    ∀ n : ℕ, 5040 &lt; n →
      ((ArithmeticFunction.sigma 1 n : ℕ) : ℝ) &lt;
        Real.exp Real.eulerMascheroniConstant * (n : ℝ) * Real.log (Real.log (n : ℝ))

theorem lagarias_inequality :
    ∀ n : ℕ, 0 &lt; n →
      ((ArithmeticFunction.sigma 1 n : ℕ) : ℝ) ≤
        (harmonic n : ℝ) + Real.exp (harmonic n : ℝ) * Real.log (harmonic n : ℝ)</pre>
