        <item>
            <title><![CDATA[Show HN: Zero C# – C# without a runtime or GC (146 pts)]]></title>
            <link>https://onurgumus.github.io/zero-csharp/index.html</link>
            <guid>50590279</guid>
            <pubDate>Sat, 21 Nov 2026 15:39:09 GMT</pubDate>
            <description><![CDATA[<p>URL: <a href="https://onurgumus.github.io/zero-csharp/index.html">https://onurgumus.github.io/zero-csharp/index.html</a>, See on <a href="https://news.ycombinator.com/item?id=50590279">Hacker News</a></p>
<div id="readability-page-1" class="page"><div data-pagefind-body="">
    <section>
      <div>
        
        <p>A fork of bflat compiles C# straight to a small native program: no .NET runtime, no garbage collector, nothing to install where it runs.</p>
        <p>You keep C#'s syntax, type checking and generics. Memory comes from arenas you free all at once, and a small library gives you strings, collections, input and output on those terms. The same source runs on Linux, macOS and Windows, on WebAssembly, and on DOS on a 386.</p>
        
      </div>
      <div>
<pre aria-label="C# example"><code><span>// Read into a stack buffer: nothing allocated per answer.</span>
<span>Span</span>&lt;<span>char</span>&gt; buffer = <span>stackalloc char</span>[<span>32</span>];

<span>Out</span>.Write(<span>$"Q</span>{i}<span>: </span>{a}<span> + </span>{b}<span> = "</span>);
<span>int</span> length = <span>In</span>.ReadLine(buffer);

<span>// Kept until the end, so it goes into an arena.</span>
mistakes.Add(<span>Str</span>.Format(keep,
    <span>$"</span>{a}<span> + </span>{b}<span> = </span>{a + b}<span>, not </span>{value}<span>"</span>));</code></pre>
        <p>From the quiz the <a href="https://onurgumus.github.io/zero-csharp/tutorial/index.html">tutorial</a> builds, step by step.</p>
      </div>
    </section>

    <div aria-labelledby="ledger-title">
        <h2 id="ledger-title">The language stays, the runtime goes</h2>
        <p>The compiler still checks everything it checks in .NET. What's missing is what a runtime would do while your program runs.</p>
        <div>
          <div>
            <h3>You keep</h3>
            <ul>
              <li><b>C# 15</b><span>Unions, extension members and the new memory safety rules.</span></li>
              <li><b>Real generics</b><span>A specialized copy for each type, and static abstract members.</span></li>
              <li><b>Interfaces and virtual methods</b><span>Default interface methods, casts, <code>is</code> and <code>as</code>.</span></li>
              <li><b>Records, tuples, anonymous types</b><span>Value equality, <code>with</code>, and the generated <code>ToString</code>.</span></li>
              <li><b>Lambdas and queries</b><span><code>Where</code>, <code>Select</code> and <code>GroupBy</code>, from a small library instead of LINQ.</span></li>
              <li><b>Threads</b><span><code>Parallel.For</code> runs a loop on every core.</span></li>
            </ul>
          </div>
          <div>
            <h3>You do without</h3>
            <ul>
              <li><b>Garbage collector</b><span>Arenas free their memory all at once, when you say.</span></li>
              <li><b>.NET runtime</b><span>Hello world is 11 KB on Windows and 40 KB on Linux, with nothing to install.</span></li>
              <li><b>Catching exceptions</b><span><code>throw</code> ends the program. Return errors as unions.</span></li>
              <li><b>Reflection and async</b><span>A type can be compared, but it has no name.</span></li>
            </ul>
          </div>
        </div>
      </div>

    <div>
        <h2>One program, five machines</h2>
        <p>The hello-world sample, unchanged, built for each target. Each build is checked by the same test suite, which requires the same output everywhere.</p>
        <div>
          <div>
<pre><span>$</span> ./build.sh Minimal
<span>$</span> ./run.sh Minimal
Hello from zero-mode!
<span>$</span> wc -c bin/Minimal
39848 bin/Minimal</pre>
            <h3>Linux, x64 or arm64</h3>
            <p>A native program that needs only the C library. On macOS it builds and runs in a container.</p>
          </div>
          <div>
<pre><span>$</span> ./build.sh --mac Minimal
<span>$</span> bin/mac/Minimal
Hello from zero-mode!
<span>$</span> wc -c bin/mac/Minimal
64728 bin/mac/Minimal</pre>
            <h3>macOS on Apple Silicon</h3>
            <p>A native Mac program: bflat compiles it, the Mac's own clang links it. No container to run it.</p>
          </div>
          <div>
<pre><span>$</span> ./build.sh --win Minimal
<span>$</span> ./run.sh --win Minimal
Hello from zero-mode!
<span>$</span> wc -c bin/win/Minimal.exe
10752 bin/win/Minimal.exe</pre>
            <h3>Windows x64</h3>
            <p>A console program on Windows' own C library, built from Linux or macOS. Tested under Wine.</p>
          </div>
          <div>
<pre><span>$</span> ./build.sh --wasm Minimal
<span>$</span> ./run.sh --wasm Minimal
Hello from zero-mode!
<span>$</span> wc -c bin/wasm/Minimal.wasm
15833 bin/wasm/Minimal.wasm</pre>
            <h3>WebAssembly</h3>
            <p>A WASI module, through NativeAOT-LLVM. Runs under wasmtime.</p>
          </div>
          <div>
<pre>C:\&gt;D:
D:\&gt;PROGRAM
<span>Hello from zero-mode!</span>

D:\&gt;DIR PROGRAM.EXE
PROGRAM  EXE     16,384

D:\&gt;</pre>
            <h3>DOS on a 386</h3>
            <p>The same bitcode compiled for a 386, run by the HX DOS extender. Tested on Bochs's i386 model.</p>
          </div>
        </div>
      </div>

    <div>
        <h2>What's different, and what you get</h2>
        <p>Without a runtime, a few things work differently. Each has a page that explains why and what to do instead.</p>
        <ul>
          <li>
            <h3>Memory is yours to free</h3>
            <p>There is no garbage collector, so <code>new</code> is never freed. Arenas hand out memory and free it all at once, one per lifetime.</p>
            <a href="https://onurgumus.github.io/zero-csharp/concepts/memory.html">Memory without a garbage collector</a>
          </li>
          <li>
            <h3>Text without throwaway strings</h3>
            <p><code>Out.Line($"...")</code> formats straight into the output buffer, and <code>In.ReadLine</code> reads into a span. Strings that must last go into an arena.</p>
            <a href="https://onurgumus.github.io/zero-csharp/concepts/strings.html">Strings without a garbage collector</a>
          </li>
          <li>
            <h3>Collections that live in arenas</h3>
            <p><code>List</code>, <code>Dict</code>, and their versions for references and string keys, all handles into an arena.</p>
            <a href="https://onurgumus.github.io/zero-csharp/how-to/choose-a-collection.html">Choose a collection</a>
          </li>
          <li>
            <h3>C# 15, with the safety rules on</h3>
            <p>Every program compiles under the updated memory safety rules: creating a pointer is safe, reading through one is marked and explained.</p>
            <a href="https://onurgumus.github.io/zero-csharp/concepts/safety-rules.html">The memory safety rules</a>
          </li>
        </ul>
      </div>

    <div>
        <h2>Where to start</h2>
        <p>If you're new to it, these four pages take you from nothing to an interactive program.</p>
        <ol>
          <li><a href="https://onurgumus.github.io/zero-csharp/get-started/install-with-dotnet.html">Install</a><p>With dotnet on Linux, or from the repository for every target.</p></li>
          <li><a href="https://onurgumus.github.io/zero-csharp/get-started/first-program.html">Your first program</a><p>Build it, run it, see how small it is.</p></li>
          <li><a href="https://onurgumus.github.io/zero-csharp/get-started/dos.html">Run it on DOS</a><p>The same program under FreeDOS, in an emulator.</p></li>
          <li><a href="https://onurgumus.github.io/zero-csharp/tutorial/index.html">The tutorial</a><p>A quiz: output, input, bad input, arenas.</p></li>
        </ol>
        <p>Looking for something specific? The <a href="https://onurgumus.github.io/zero-csharp/how-to/index.html">task guides</a> answer one question each, and the <a href="https://onurgumus.github.io/zero-csharp/reference/index.html">reference</a> lists every type in the library.</p>
      </div>
  </div></div>]]></description>
        </item>
