      <h2>ClawDOS</h2>

<p>
  <b>An AI coding agent for DOS.</b><br>
  It runs on an IBM PC XT: an 8088 at 4.77 MHz, 640K of RAM, a CGA card.
</p>

<div>

  <blockquote>
    <p><strong>A (real) human’s note:</strong></p>

    <hr>

    <p><strong>Backstory:</strong> The IBM PC was my first computer. Rather, it was my parents’ first personal computer that I got to use at a very early age.</p>

    <p>It was already obsolete by the time I was old enough to use it, but at 5 years old it was a magical device that I could make do different things depending on what I typed.</p>

    <p>I learned how to type and use DOS so that I could launch some early “Learn the Alphabet” games that my mom had bought me. I learned some elementary-level use of BASIC, but at the time that didn’t progress much further than making some number guessing games and other small projects.</p>

    <p>From there, many years passed but I still have some of the original parts of that PC. I have a collection of vintage machines that mean something to me, with my fully maxed-out IBM PC 5160 being the centerpiece of my collection. It still has a working 20 MB hard drive, but that’s disconnected in favor of a PicoMEM that lets me emulate a hard drive off of an SD card, making experimentation and play much easier.</p>

    <p><strong>Old, meet new. New, meet old:</strong> I love intersections of old and new technology. What better way to cross the two than building an AI coding agent to run on DOS on original hardware?</p>

    <p>And of course, what better to build it than an AI coding agent?</p>

    <p>I (read: Claude) built this for my own entertainment. I wanted to see how far I could push an AI coding agent to build something given out of the (modern) norm of constraints. Also, can that something it built then build other somethings? 100% of this repo is written by Claude, with the exception of the text within this box.</p>

    <p>As explained in more detail below, the harness runs on the native OS and hardware and relies on a “bridge” PC in order to handle TLS, which would’ve been more than what an 8088 could handle.</p>
  </blockquote>

</div>

<p><img src="http://ricapar.net/ClawDOS/docs/images/opening.png" alt="The ClawDOS opening screen" width="640"></p>

<p>Type <code>CLAWDOS</code> at the <code>C:\&gt;</code> prompt and talk to Claude. It reads and edits the
files on your disk, writes programs, compiles them with Turbo C, runs them and
looks at what they draw on the screen. It keeps notes between conversations and
picks up where it left off.</p>

<p>The whole harness runs on the PC. <code>HARNESS.EXE</code> is a 176 KB DOS program that
speaks the Claude Messages API itself: it writes the requests, parses the
streamed reply, runs the tools and keeps every conversation on its own disk.
The 8088 does everything but TLS.</p>

<h2 id="see-it-work">See it work</h2>

<p>Every screen below comes from a 5160 at 4.77 MHz, emulated in 86Box. The
conversations are with the live API.</p>

<h3 id="from-a-sentence-to-a-running-program">From a sentence to a running program</h3>

<p><img src="http://ricapar.net/ClawDOS/docs/images/coding.png" alt="ClawDOS writes, compiles and runs PRIMES.C" width="640"></p>

<p>One message: <em>“Write PRIMES.C: print the primes below 100. Compile it with
Turbo C and run it.”</em> ClawDOS reads its guide to the compilers on this machine,
writes the source, then compiles, links and runs it in a single tool call. The
answer is on the screen 64 seconds after Enter, 26 of them spent compiling,
linking and running.</p>

<p>The compiler gets the machine to itself. For a build, ClawDOS saves its state
and leaves memory, Turbo C runs with about 450K free, and the harness comes
back on the same conversation with the program’s output in hand.</p>

<h3 id="wargames-written-on-the-xt">WarGames, written on the XT</h3>

<p>ClawDOS wrote this tribute to the 1983 film on the PC itself: a vector world
map, missiles arcing over the pole, a tic-tac-toe endgame, and a score for the
PC speaker or an AdLib card. Five files of Turbo C, 15 KB of source, a
<code>WG.EXE</code> under 10 KB.</p>

<p>
  <img src="http://ricapar.net/ClawDOS/docs/images/wargames-1.png" alt="GREETINGS PROFESSOR FALKEN. SHALL WE PLAY A GAME?" width="49%">
  <img src="http://ricapar.net/ClawDOS/docs/images/wargames-2.png" alt="Global Thermonuclear War: missile arcs over a world map" width="49%">
</p>
<p>
  <img src="http://ricapar.net/ClawDOS/docs/images/wargames-3.png" alt="Tic-tac-toe games played to a draw" width="49%">
  <img src="http://ricapar.net/ClawDOS/docs/images/wargames-4.png" alt="The only winning move is not to play" width="49%">
</p>

<p>Here it is running on the real 5160, filmed off its IBM monitor:</p>

<p>
  <a href="https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=Jok7MQi-16Q"><img src="http://ricapar.net/ClawDOS/docs/images/wargames-video.jpg" alt="Video: the WarGames demo running on a real IBM PC 5160 (YouTube)" width="640"></a><br>
  <a href="https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=Jok7MQi-16Q">▶ Watch the WarGames demo on YouTube</a>
</p>

<h3 id="it-remembers">It remembers</h3>

<p>Start a new conversation and “your WarGames demo” is all it needs. ClawDOS
opens the note it saved in <code>C:\MEMORY</code>, finds the latest build, and recalls
the palette trick the war scene plays on the CGA.</p>

<p><img src="http://ricapar.net/ClawDOS/docs/images/wargames-run.png" alt="ClawDOS reads its WARGAMES memory and runs the demo with a screenshot" width="640"></p>

<h3 id="it-sees-the-screen">It sees the screen</h3>

<p>That <code>Run</code> call asked for a screenshot 25 seconds in. A timer-interrupt hook
copies the CGA’s video memory at that moment, the 8088 encodes it as a PNG, and
the picture goes to Claude with the tool result. This is the file the PC made,
320x200 in four colours, 16 KB:</p>

<p><img src="http://ricapar.net/ClawDOS/docs/images/wargames-shot-8088.png" alt="The PNG screenshot encoded on the 8088" width="320"></p>

<p>And this is what Claude made of it:</p>

<p><img src="http://ricapar.net/ClawDOS/docs/images/wargames-seen.png" alt="Claude describes the screenshot: the map, the missile arcs, the explosions" width="640"></p>

<h3 id="an-agent-at-work">An agent at work</h3>

<p>Asked to make the demo exit on its own, ClawDOS goes looking. It searches the
sources for the keyboard wait, reads the file, says what it found and edits one
line. Each tool call shows in the transcript with its result, and between them
Claude says what it is about to do.</p>

<p><img src="http://ricapar.net/ClawDOS/docs/images/agent-at-work.png" alt="Search, Read and Edit tool calls in the transcript" width="640"></p>

<h3 id="it-plays">It plays</h3>

<p>Text art in sixteen colours, straight into the transcript, and music on the PC
speaker in GW-BASIC’s <code>PLAY</code> language.</p>

<p><img src="http://ricapar.net/ClawDOS/docs/images/textart.png" alt="ClawDOS draws itself in coloured text art" width="640"></p>

<h2 id="the-harness">The harness</h2>

<p><strong>A chat screen built for the CGA.</strong> Replies stream onto the screen as they
arrive, word-wrapped at 80 columns in code page 437. Writes are timed to the
retrace, so there is no snow. The transcript scrolls in hardware through the
CRTC start address, with PgUp and PgDn to go back. The spinner and its seconds
run from the timer interrupt and keep moving while the PC is busy.</p>

<p><strong>Eighteen tools.</strong> Claude works on the machine through these:</p>

<table>
  <thead>
    <tr>
      <th>Tools</th>
      <th>What they do</th>
    </tr>
  </thead>
  <tbody>
    <tr>
      <td><code>sysinfo</code>, <code>dir</code>, <code>read</code>, <code>search</code></td>
      <td>Look at the system, list directories, read files as text or hex, find text across files</td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
      <td><code>write</code>, <code>edit</code>, <code>delete</code>, <code>mkdir</code>, <code>rename</code></td>
      <td>Create and change files; <code>edit</code> replaces one piece of a file in place</td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
      <td><code>run</code></td>
      <td>Run DOS programs and batch files and read back what they printed, up to twelve commands in a call, with a screenshot on request</td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
      <td><code>peek</code>, <code>poke</code>, <code>port</code></td>
      <td>Read and write memory and I/O ports</td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
      <td><code>play</code>, <code>textart</code></td>
      <td>PC speaker music and coloured CP437 art</td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
      <td><code>memory_list</code>, <code>memory_read</code>, <code>memory_write</code></td>
      <td>Its own notes, kept in <code>C:\MEMORY</code></td>
    </tr>
  </tbody>
</table>

<p><strong>A development kit on the disk.</strong> Two assemblers (TINYASM and WASM),
Bruce’s C, GW-BASIC and the lDebug debugger, plus Turbo C 2.0, installed from
your own floppy images in <code>external/</code>. All of them are proven on the 8088, and
ClawDOS knows how to drive each one.</p>

<p><strong>Memories and skills.</strong> Memories are what ClawDOS learns on your PC: what you
built together, what worked, what you like. Skills are guides that ship with
it, such as the dev tools and CGA demo effects. Both are plain text files on
the disk, listed in every conversation’s system prompt and read when they fit
the task. F4 browses the memories:</p>

<p><img src="http://ricapar.net/ClawDOS/docs/images/memories.png" alt="The F4 memories screen" width="640"></p>

<p><strong>Safe mode.</strong> On by default. Anything that changes a file, memory or a port
asks <code>Allow ...? (y/n)</code> first. F3 turns it off.</p>

<p><strong>Conversations that last.</strong> Every chat is a file on the disk in the API’s own
message format. F6 lists them, Enter reopens one, and your next message
carries it on. A chat cut off by the power switch reopens too.</p>

<p><img src="http://ricapar.net/ClawDOS/docs/images/chats.png" alt="The F6 chat list" width="640"></p>

<p><strong>Any model, mid-conversation.</strong> <code>/model</code> lists the providers in
<code>CLAWDOS.CFG</code> and switches between them and their models without losing the
conversation. F5 sets how hard Claude thinks, from low to max. Any host that
speaks the Messages API works.</p>

<p><strong>Easy on a slow link.</strong> The system prompt and tools are cached by the API.
After a conversation’s first request the PC uploads only what is new, so a tool
result goes up in about 500 bytes. A dropped connection or an overloaded API
is retried, and the turn carries on.</p>

<p><strong>A flight recorder.</strong> <code>CLAWDOS.LOG</code> has a line for every round: time to first
token, upload size and time, tokens in and out, cache reads.</p>

<div><pre><code>api anthropic claude-opus-5-5 round=1 ttft=2750 total=4015 up=165 len=1784 stop=tool_use in=2 out=221 cr=5543 cw=739 th=cont
relaunch: program 61.5 s, CLAWDOS away 82.3 s
</code></pre></div>

<h2 id="under-the-hood">Under the hood</h2>

<pre><code>flowchart LR
    subgraph DOS["IBM PC XT (8088, 640K, CGA)"]
        direction TB
        subgraph APP["HARNESS.EXE"]
            direction TB
            UI["Chat screen"]
            LOOP["Agent loop&lt;br/&gt;Messages API requests, SSE and JSON,&lt;br/&gt;history on disk"]
            TOOLS["Tools&lt;br/&gt;files, search, DOS programs, screenshots,&lt;br/&gt;memory and ports, speaker"]
        end
        NET["mTCP + packet driver"]
        APP &lt;--&gt; NET
    end

    TUNNEL["TLS tunnel&lt;br/&gt;copies bytes"]
    API["Claude Messages API"]

    NET &lt;--&gt;|"plain HTTP"| TUNNEL
    TUNNEL &lt;--&gt;|"HTTPS"| API
</code></pre>

<table>
  <thead>
