    </tr>
    <tr>
      <td><strong>Code</strong></td>
      <td>13,000 lines of C and C++, built with Open Watcom for the 8086 instruction set</td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
      <td><strong>Programs</strong></td>
      <td><code>HARNESS.EXE</code>, 176 KB, the whole harness. <code>CLAWDOS.EXE</code>, 29 KB, the launcher you type</td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
      <td><strong>System</strong></td>
      <td>FreeDOS 1.4 with mTCP for TCP/IP</td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
      <td><strong>API</strong></td>
      <td>The Claude Messages API, streamed: tool use, images, prompt caching, message threads</td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
      <td><strong>TLS</strong></td>
      <td>A tunnel on another machine, <code>tunnel/tunnel.py</code>, <code>socat</code> or <code>stunnel</code>. It copies bytes between a plain port and <code>api.anthropic.com:443</code>. It can also hold the API key, so the key never touches the PC’s disk</td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
      <td><strong>Memory</strong></td>
      <td>A program ClawDOS runs gets 200K beside the harness, or 450K when the harness steps out</td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
      <td><strong>Tests</strong></td>
      <td>31 boot tests in DOSBox-X against a mock API, unit tests that run on DOS, and runs on 86Box’s 8088</td>
    </tr>
  </tbody>
</table>

<p><a href="http://ricapar.net/ClawDOS/docs/API.html">docs/API.md</a> has exactly what ClawDOS sends and what it expects back.</p>

<h2 id="run-it">Run it</h2>

<p>You need a Mac or a Windows PC with Python 3, and an Anthropic API key. On
Windows the command is <code>python</code>.</p>

<div><pre><code>python3 scripts/setup-tools.py          # fetch the compilers, FreeDOS, mTCP and the emulators
echo ANTHROPIC_API_KEY=sk-ant-... &gt; .env
python3 scripts/build.py                # build/CLAWDOS.IMG, a bootable disk
python3 tunnel/tunnel.py                # the TLS tunnel, on port 8443
python3 scripts/run86box.py             # boot an IBM PC XT
</code></pre></div>

<p>At the <code>C:\&gt;</code> prompt, type <code>CLAWDOS</code>.</p>

<p>No key? <code>python3 tunnel/mockapi.py</code> plays the API offline: type
<code>CLAWDOS 10.0.2.2:18023</code> and send <code>!help</code>.</p>

<p><strong>On a real PC:</strong> build with the address of the machine running the tunnel
(<code>python3 scripts/build.py --tunnel 192.168.1.10:8443</code>), copy <code>CLAWDOS.IMG</code> to
the PicoMEM’s microSD card, pick it as the hard disk in the PicoMEM’s menu,
boot, and type <code>CLAWDOS</code>. Later builds update the card’s image in place and
keep its memories and chats
(<code>python3 scripts/build.py --update /path/to/CLAWDOS.IMG</code>).</p>

